WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:18.789
Because of the ship's tilt, the bay was below me; Israel Hands had fallen between me and the rest of the ship .

2
00:00:18.790 --> 00:00:24.657
He rose to the surface in a lather of foam and blood, and then bobbed in the waves.

3
00:00:24.658 --> 00:00:34.783
I felt sick, faint, and terrified. The dagger burned hot in my shoulder, and I clung to the ropes with both hands until my fingernails ached.

4
00:00:34.784 --> 00:00:43.015
I wanted to be free from the mast, but I thought falling into the water beside the dead body of Israel Hands would be far worse!

5
00:00:43.016 --> 00:00:46.337
I could feel hot blood running over my back and chest.

6
00:00:46.338 --> 00:00:52.971
"Pull out the knife!" I told myself. But neither of my hands would let go of the ropes.

7
00:00:52.972 --> 00:00:58.004
I started to cry and shuddered at the thought of my predicament.

8
00:00:58.005 --> 00:01:07.308
Oddly enough, my shuddering helped. The knife, which I thought had stuck me to the mast so securely, had really almost missed me completely.

9
00:01:07.309 --> 00:01:13.145
I was held to the mast only by a mere pinch of skin, and my shudder tore it away.

10
00:01:13.146 --> 00:01:17.231
Blood ran down my back faster, but I was free.

11
00:01:17.232 --> 00:01:24.541
I pulled my coat and shirt away, moved to the opposite side of the ship, and climbed down on that side.

12
00:01:24.542 --> 00:01:28.722
Nothing could make me go down the ropes above where Israel had fallen.

13
00:01:28.723 --> 00:01:32.579
I went below deck and did what I could for my wound.

14
00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:39.681
I was in pain, but the wound was neither deep nor dangerous, nor did it bother me too much to use my arm.

15
00:01:39.682 --> 00:01:43.796
Back up on the top deck, I looked around and saw Red Cap.

16
00:01:43.797 --> 00:01:49.205
He was leaning against the side of the ship like some horrible, life-size puppet.

17
00:01:49.206 --> 00:01:53.481
"He looks easier to move now," I said to myself.

18
00:01:53.482 --> 00:02:02.007
After what I had been through, another dead body didn't bother me, so I grabbed his belt and, with one good heave, threw him overboard.

19
00:02:02.008 --> 00:02:07.945
He went in with a splash, but his red cap came off and remained floating in the water.

20
00:02:07.946 --> 00:02:13.767
The evening breeze started whistling softly through the rope lines and rattled the sails.

21
00:02:13.768 --> 00:02:17.442
"The wind may be a danger to the ship," I thought.

22
00:02:17.443 --> 00:02:22.709
I was able to get the front sails down, but the mainsail was too difficult to manage.

23
00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:29.122
When the ship tipped, the mainsail had swung out so that now a foot or two of the sail was under water.

24
00:02:29.123 --> 00:02:36.695
I got my knife and cut the lines that usually raise and lower the sail, and the loose canvas dropped instantly into the water.

25
00:02:36.696 --> 00:02:42.212
"Now both the Hispaniola and I must hope for a little luck," I said.

26
00:02:42.213 --> 00:02:49.252
I suddenly realized the sun had set and the air was getting chilly, so I let myself drop softly overboard.

27
00:02:49.253 --> 00:02:55.453
The water barely reached my waist, and I waded ashore as the breeze whistled low among the pines.

28
00:02:55.454 --> 00:03:00.656
I wanted to get to my friends and boast that I had recaptured the ship single-handedly.

29
00:03:00.657 --> 00:03:04.883
"Even Captain Smollett will admit I'm resourceful and brave," I thought.

30
00:03:04.884 --> 00:03:11.284
"He may even want to give me a medal or something!" I laughed out loud at that.

31
00:03:11.285 --> 00:03:18.802
In high spirits, I set out for the stockade, and before long, I was near the hill where I'd first encountered Ben Gunn.

32
00:03:18.803 --> 00:03:25.020
In the distance, I could see a glow against the sky and thought Ben Gunn was cooking his supper.

33
00:03:25.021 --> 00:03:31.614
"Why is he so careless?" I wondered. "If I can see his fire, so can Long John."

34
00:03:31.615 --> 00:03:33.998
Gradually the night became darker.

35
00:03:33.999 --> 00:03:40.980
I kept tripping over tree roots and stumbling into sandpits until the moon finally rose and gave me some light.

36
00:03:40.981 --> 00:03:45.602
I continued walking, sometimes running impatiently, to the stockade.

37
00:03:45.603 --> 00:03:51.964
"I must stay alert," I thought. "I don't want my friends to shoot me by mistake."

38
00:03:51.965 --> 00:03:56.743
As the moon climbed higher, the glow appeared among the trees again.

39
00:03:56.744 --> 00:04:03.488
This time, the fire resembled the red-hot embers of a smoldering bonfire somewhere near the stockade.

40
00:04:03.489 --> 00:04:11.340
By the time I reached the fence, I saw the remains of what must have been an immense fire at the side of the house.

41
00:04:11.341 --> 00:04:14.976
"Did something go wrong while I was absent?" I wondered.

42
00:04:14.977 --> 00:04:21.523
I climbed the fence, got on my hands and knees, and crawled without a sound toward the corner of the house.

43
00:04:21.524 --> 00:04:30.319
As I drew near, I heard my friends snoring, peaceful in their sleep, and the unpleasant noises sounded like music to me.

44
00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:37.287
"If Long John was creeping in on them like this, my friends would all be dead by daybreak," I said to myself.

45
00:04:37.288 --> 00:04:42.002
"I shouldn't have left them with so few men remaining to guard the house."

46
00:04:42.003 --> 00:04:43.186
I crept in.

47
00:04:43.187 --> 00:04:51.233
"I should lie down in my usual place," I thought with a chuckle, "and enjoy the looks on their faces when they find me in the morning."

48
00:04:51.234 --> 00:04:56.243
My foot struck a sleeper's leg, and he turned and groaned, but didn't wake.

49
00:04:56.244 --> 00:05:01.687
And then suddenly a shrill voice screamed from the darkness.

50
00:05:01.688 --> 00:05:06.599
"Pieces of eight! Pieces of eight! Pieces of eight!"

51
00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:14.487
"Long John's parrot!" I whispered. My body froze with fear as the sleepers awoke and jumped up.

52
00:05:14.488 --> 00:05:18.448
"Who goes there?" roared Long John Silver.

53
00:05:18.449 --> 00:05:25.228
I turned to run, slammed violently against one person, jumped back, and ran into the arms o f a second man.

54
00:05:25.229 --> 00:05:28.756
He held me tight as I struggled against his grip.

55
00:05:28.757 --> 00:05:32.274
"Bring a torch, Dick," said Long John.

56
00:05:32.275 --> 00:05:41.345
One of the pirates left the log house and returned with a long piece of burning wood.
